## Test Structure
- Write unit tests for individual components
- Implement feature tests for end-to-end functionality
- Structure tests to match your application architecture
- Create dedicated test classes for each feature or component
- Follow naming conventions for test methods (test_it_does_something)
- Organize tests in appropriate directories (Unit, Feature, Integration)

## Test Data
- Use Laravel's testing helpers and factories to generate test data
- Implement model factories for all models used in tests
- Use database transactions to isolate tests
- Avoid hard-coded test data values
- Use data providers for testing multiple scenarios
- Create reusable traits for common test data setup

## Test Isolation
- Keep tests independent and idempotent
- Reset application state between tests
- Use database transactions to prevent test pollution
- Mock external services and dependencies
- Use setUp and tearDown methods appropriately
- Isolate tests from production environment settings

## Test Coverage
- Use test coverage tools to identify untested areas
- Aim for high test coverage of critical business logic
- Test both happy paths and edge cases
- Write tests for reported bugs to prevent regression
- Establish minimum test coverage requirements for CI/CD
- Balance testing effort with development velocity

## Test Performance
- Optimize slow tests by using in-memory databases when possible
- Use parallel testing for large test suites
- Limit the use of heavyweight browser tests to essential flows
- Favor unit tests over feature tests when appropriate
- Use test doubles (mocks, stubs) to speed up tests
- Implement proper database seeding strategies

## Test Maintenance
- Regularly run and update tests as part of the development process
- Refactor tests when application code changes
- Keep test code clean and maintainable
- Document complex test scenarios
- Use continuous integration to run tests automatically
- Review test failures promptly and fix both tests and code
